MOLECULAR HYDROGEN DISSOCIATION TO N=3
h2dis_n3.nc

  --Version 2.0--
This file was generated from a combination of V. 2.1 of h2pdisrec.nc and a
run of ratecalc from h2dis_n3.input to set up the independent and dependent 
variable arrays. Significant manual editing was required fill in the 
placeholder values. The bulk of the reaction rate values were computed by a 
run of ratecalc using the Janev book fits. The initial six values violated 
the fit constraints. A forced evaluation of the fit in KaleidaGraph yields 
reasonable values here, so they have been pasted in. The same was done for 
the last 4 values. The background energy loss (21.5 * reaction rate) comes 
directly from the Janev book. The dissociation energy for this reaction is more
complicated than the others. The paper by Higo (M. Higo et al., Chem.
Phys. 73, 99 (1982); see also D. P. Stotler et al., Phys. Plasmas 3, 
4084 (1996)) reported a bimodal distribution of dissociation energies,
varying from mostly slow (0.25 eV) at low electron energies (near the 
threshold of 19 eV) to almost 50-50 slow and fast (6.7 eV) for electron
energies of 90 eV. The energies were converted into equivalent electron
temperatures by dividing by 3/2. The distribution of energies was effected
by manually setting up a 2-D table with one dimension being the electron
temperature (6 values, 10 to 60 eV), the other representing a random
variable between 0 and 1. The H-alpha emission rate was computed from
the dissociation rate as: \[
\frac{A_{3 \rightarrow 2}}{( A_{3 \rightarrow 2} + A_{3 \rightarrow 1}
)} E_{3 \rightarrow 2} S_{dis},
\]
where: $A_{3 \rightarrow 2} = 4.410 \times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$,
$A_{3 \rightarrow 1} = 5.575 \times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$
(this ratio was previously set to $0.439$ following Reiter's 1992 PSI),
$E_{3 \rightarrow 2} = (1/4 - 1/9) \times 13.595$ eV. Finally, the
H-alpha wavelengths appear as 3 separate dependent variables with nonzero
values only in a narrow range for the emitter mass around the appropriate 
isotope mass.

