MOLECULAR HYDROGEN ION DISSOCIATION TO N=3
h2pdis_n3.nc

  --Version 2.0--
The file was generated manually using h2pdisrec.nc as a template. The
rate in the Janev book and Aladdin database (Janev reaction 2.2.13) is
for a final state of n=2. Following reasoning described in Reiter et
al., J. Nucl. Mater. 196-198, 1059 (1992), that rate is multiplied by
a factor of 0.174 to estimate the rate for reactions resulting in an
n=3 state.  A few values at the extremes of the temperature range
exceed the fit restrictions. However, the resulting rate values are
reasonable and are included here anyway.  The electron energy loss per
reaction is taken directly from the Janev book as 17.5 eV (i.e., the
background energy loss rate is 17.5 * reaction rate).  In principle it
should probably be about 1 eV larger (because of a higher lying
intermediate state), but have no definite calculation from which we
can obtain an alternative value. The dissociation energy is set at 1.5 eV,
as specified in the Janev book. The H-alpha emission rate was computed from
the dissociation rate as: \[
\frac{A_{3 \rightarrow 2}}{( A_{3 \rightarrow 2} + A_{3 \rightarrow 1}
)} E_{3 \rightarrow 2} S_{dis},
\]
where: $A_{3 \rightarrow 2} = 4.410 \times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$,
$A_{3 \rightarrow 1} = 5.575 \times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$
(this ratio was previously set to $0.439$ following Reiter's 1992 PSI),
$E_{3 \rightarrow 2} = (1/4 - 1/9) \times 13.595$ eV. Finally, the
H-alpha wavelengths appear as 3 separate dependent variables with nonzero
values only in a narrow range for the emitter mass around the appropriate 
isotope mass.

