ATOMIC DEUTERIUM ION ELASTIC SCATTERING
dd_00_elastic.nc

    --Version 3.1--
These data were produced by ratecalc, revision 1.34, using
dd_elastic.input, revision 1.1, as the input file.  The fits in
dd_elastic.input were based on data obtained directly from
P. S. Krstic of the CFADC.  The first four energy decades (1.e-3 -> 10
eV) were computed with their quantum mechanical code. The fifth energy
decade (10 -> 100 eV) uses the results of their semi-classical
calculations. Except for the first two decades, all of these data are
now available on their Web site, http://www-cfadc.phy.ornl.gov. The
scattering_angle probability distribution was computed from the
CFADC data using a separate code. That code and the original data
are stored in the CVS archive "elastic" on the PPPL Unix cluster. The
"00" means that the assumed minimum scattering angle is 0 degrees, and
no approximations have been made in computing the cross sections.
These data are provided for comparison purposes with the "05" data.
Additional information on these data is contained in the
dd_elastic.input file.

Ratecalc was run in DEBUG mode to produce this file. Because of the
highly oscillatory nature of some of the integrands the accuracy
parameter, integration_acc, in ratecalc.hweb had to be increased to
const(1.5,-6) for ratecalc to converge. The netCDF file was then
edited by hand to:

1. Change the units for "scattering_angle" to "radians",
2. Change the units for "1st_random_number" to "     " (i.e., no units),
3. Change the units for "angle_min" to "radians",
4. Set sigv_max to 4e-07 (second of the three zeroes at the end of the
   file).

NOTE: by virtue of the quantum mechanical indistinguishability of
the products in this reaction, this reaction is really a "total"
scattering which incorporates the classically defined elastic and
charge exchange processes. Hence, when using this reaction in DEGAS 2,
the user should not add a separate charge exchange reaction. 

NOTE: because these data are based upon a differential scattering
cross section which does not scale with the relative velocity of the
reagents, they cannot be used for other species. Once "species-specific"
reactions have been enabled in DEGAS 2, this caveat will be enforced
automatically by the code.

