ELECTRON IMPACT IONIZATION OF ARGON
arionize.nc

     --Version 1.0--
This file can be generated from the Aladdin format data in argon.dat
(in degas2/Aladdin/data). These fit coefficients were taken from EIRENE's
AMJUEL file (this version was dated 1/20/99). Comments there indicate that
they originated in Behringer's STRAHL code, with an introduction date 
of 1/96. Although these coefficients are in the familiar Janev format,
no restrictions are given on the temperature range. No bizarre behavior
is noted over the usual 1 -> 5.e4 range, so that has been used here.
The reaction rate compares well with the one in the atmc.dat file and
with a simpler one quoted by Nichols and Manos, J. Appl. Phys. 80 (1996),
2643. Note that AMJUEL also had a fit for the energy loss per reaction of
15.8 eV (constant), roughly the ionization energy. This loss rate differs
from that only at the edges of the fit range used here (not sure if this is 
right).

ARGON CHARGE EXCHANGE
archex.nc

     --Version 1.0--
This file can be generated from the Aladdin format data in argon.dat
(in degas2/Aladdin/data). These fit coefficients are equivalent to the
simple power law decay fit sigma = 5.19e-15 * E**(-0.152) quoted by
Nichols and Manos (J. Appl. Phys. 80 (1996) 2643). This is a fit to
earlier experimental data. At least one source (Physics Vade Mecum)
shows different cross sections, especially above a few eV. However,
these were not sufficiently well documented to permit their use. 
As a result, a search for better data should be undertaken. Furthermore,
the simple power law fit does not give the expected logarithmic
divergence at low energies. The power law fit also leads to a 
sigma-v which is monotonically increasing over the full energy range.
Hence, the rejection technique in subr. chargex will break for energies
above the maximum. The original cross section data were valid over 
a few eV to 400 eV. The energy and temperature ranges here were taken
to be that as well. These data will be replaced once more recent
cross section fits are identified.

